\ At this point, we have a core set of system words and are in interpreter
\ mode. However, we don't yet have the ability to define new words. We'll
\ manually construct a new word, ":Def", which will allow us to define new
\ words.

'syshere @ 4 AlignTo 'syshere !      \ Align the heap pointer.
Tok :Def                             \ Tokenize the word: ":Def".
Dup 1 + Swap @bu #b,                 \ Copy the token to the heap.
'syshere @ 4 AlignTo 'syshere !      \ Align the heap again.
$0000 h,                              \ Set code len to 0 for now.
$00 b,                               \ No flags. (visible, non-immediate)
'systok @ @bu b,                     \ Copy the token length.
'syshere @ 'sysdict @ , 'sysdict !   \ Link the new word in the dictionary.

\ Now we've manually defined the header of ":Def" in the dictionary, we'll
\ define the code of the word to effectively do the same thing we just did
\ above.

\ The main notable change is the use of "Defer," to defer the execution of
\ "compilation words" (i.e. words that write to the heap and end with ",")
\ This is necessary as compilation words are "immediate" words: Words that are
\ executed as soon as they are encountered during compilation. "Defer,", being
\ a compilation word itself, writes the code to execute the next word
\ regardless of whether it is immediate or not.

] \ Exit interpreter mode.

\ All non-immediate words from here on will be compiled into the body of
\ ":Def".

    'syshere @ 4 AlignTo 'syshere !
    Tok
    Dup 1 + Swap @bu Defer, #b,
    'syshere @ 4 AlignTo 'syshere !
    $0000 Defer, h,
    $00 Defer, b,
    'systok @ @bu Defer, b,
    'syshere @ 'sysdict @ Defer, , 'sysdict !
    Exit,

[ \ Re-Enter interpreter mode.

\ We also want to go back and fix up the 16-bit code address we wrote
\ earlier. We want it to be the length of the code for ":Def", which starts
\ just after the header we wrote above, and ends at the current heap pointer.

'sysdict @ 4 - @hu
'syshere @ 'sysdict @ 4 + -
'sysdict @ 4 - !h

\ Now, ":Def" will be able to take the next token and define a new word.
\ We can now use ":Def" to define some helper words for manipulating a word's
\ header. We'll start with a word to update the code length.

:Def FixLen;
]
    'sysdict @ 4 - @hu
    'syshere @ 'sysdict @ 4 + -
    'sysdict @ 4 - !h
    Exit,
[

\ And we'll call it now to fix up the code length of "FixLen;".
FixLen;

\ Get the address of the last-defined word.
:Def :Last
]
    'sysdict @
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

\ Mark the most recently defined word as hidden.
\ Hidden words will be skipped when searching the word list.
:Def :Hidden
]
    :Last 2 - Dup \ Dup address of the flags byte.
    @b $01 BitOr  \ Set hidden flag.
    Swap !b       \ Write back flags.
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

\ Mark the most recently defined word as visible (not hidden).
:Def :Visible
]

    :Last 2 - Dup \ Dup address of the flags byte.
    @b $FE BitAnd \ Reset hidden flag.
    Swap !b       \ Write back flags.
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

\ Mark the most recently defined word as immediate.
:Def :Immediate
]
    :Last 2 - Dup \ Dup address of the flags byte.
    @b $02 BitOr  \ Set immediate flag.
    Swap !b       \ Write back flags.
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

\ Define a new hidden word and enter compilation mode.
:Def :Fn
]
    :Def :Hidden
    Defer, ]
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

\ Mark the most recently defined word as visible, compile an "Exit," to end
\ the function, and return to interpreter mode.
:Def ; :Immediate
]
    :Visible
    Defer, Exit,
    Defer, [
    FixLen;
    Exit,
[
FixLen;

:Fn test 2 2 + ;

test Panic

$4B4B $000B8000 !h
$4B4B $000B8002 !h

